WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.570
At the print shop, jobs travel between desks in envelopes.

2
00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:11.307
A clerk opens one, sees a paper size inside, and knows the job has one.

3
00:00:11.307 --> 00:00:13.509
The next clerk doesn't know that.

4
00:00:13.509 --> 00:00:15.377
She'd have to open it again.

5
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.213
Unless the first clerk stamps the outside: checked, paper size filled in.

6
00:00:20.213 --> 00:00:24.130
Then the next clerk trusts the stamp and gets on with the job.

7
00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.647
Keep that stamp in mind.

8
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:28.235
Now the shop's app.

9
00:00:28.235 --> 00:00:32.504
Some printers report their paper size, and some don't.

10
00:00:32.504 --> 00:00:41.516
So lookupPaper of laser-1 returns a String?: a paper size, such as a4, or null, Kotlin's word for no value at all.

11
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:44.974
Before printing, the app must be sure there is one.

12
00:00:44.974 --> 00:00:46.753
Write: if paper is not null.

13
00:00:46.753 --> 00:00:50.566
Inside that if, the compiler treats paper as a plain String.

14
00:00:50.566 --> 00:00:51.710
You wrote no cast.

15
00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.268
The compiler made one for you, because it saw the check.

16
00:00:55.268 --> 00:00:56.984
That's called a smart cast.

17
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.289
The standard library's require does the same job in one line.

18
00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:06.451
After require, paper not null, the next line can use paper as a plain String.

19
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:10.907
So let's write our own check, requirePaperPlain.

20
00:01:10.907 --> 00:01:12.593
If paper is null, throw.

21
00:01:12.593 --> 00:01:15.686
Then, on the next line, paper dot uppercase.

22
00:01:15.686 --> 00:01:16.810
Does it compile?

23
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:17.935
Pause and guess.

24
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:21.936
It doesn't.

25
00:01:21.936 --> 00:01:29.672
The compiler says only safe or non-null asserted calls are allowed on a nullable receiver of type String?.

26
00:01:29.672 --> 00:01:32.737
In plain words: paper might still be null.

27
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:36.733
Here's the question that's easy to be shy about.

28
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:39.800
The check is right there, and it throws on null.

29
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.461
require gets away with it.

30
00:01:41.461 --> 00:01:43.059
So why can't my function?

31
00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:47.673
Because the compiler is a clerk who trusts her own eyes.

32
00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:50.707
She saw the check in the function she's reading?

33
00:01:50.707 --> 00:01:51.655
Then she knows.

34
00:01:51.655 --> 00:01:53.868
A colleague did it at another desk?

35
00:01:53.868 --> 00:01:56.712
As far as she's concerned, it never happened.

36
00:01:56.712 --> 00:01:58.988
She never opens a function you call.

37
00:01:58.988 --> 00:02:00.884
Its body is a sealed envelope.

38
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:02.889
And that's fair.

39
00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:09.763
If smart casts followed bodies, deleting one throw could quietly change which code compiles far away.

40
00:02:09.763 --> 00:02:15.004
And often there's no body to read: a library arrives as compiled class files.

41
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.253
So we need a stamp on the outside.

42
00:02:18.253 --> 00:02:26.865
In Kotlin, that's a contract: a contract block, written as the first statement of a function, that states a fact for callers to rely on.

43
00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:33.599
returns implies paper not null says: if this function returns normally, paper was not null.

44
00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:37.045
Add it to our check, and the next line compiles.

45
00:02:37.045 --> 00:02:39.055
The demo prints: paper = A4.

46
00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:45.216
Contracts are still Experimental in Kotlin 2.4, which means the feature may still change.

47
00:02:45.216 --> 00:02:49.294
So you agree in writing, with OptIn of ExperimentalContracts.

48
00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:53.239
Leave that out, and you get: this declaration needs opt-in.

49
00:02:54.333 --> 00:02:56.379
Java never meets this problem.

50
00:02:56.379 --> 00:03:01.152
Its types don't track null at all, so it compiles, as everything does.

51
00:03:01.152 --> 00:03:02.379
That's not safety.

52
00:03:02.379 --> 00:03:03.811
It's just not asking.

53
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.108
There's a second kind of stamp.

54
00:03:07.108 --> 00:03:13.377
callsInPlace, block, EXACTLY_ONCE says: this lambda runs exactly once, during this call.

55
00:03:13.377 --> 00:03:20.216
So a val declared without a value can be assigned inside it, and the demo prints label = job-17.

56
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.947
Now let's be suspicious.

57
00:03:22.947 --> 00:03:24.388
Who checks the stamp?

58
00:03:24.388 --> 00:03:28.506
A job event is either Printed, with a page count, or Jammed.

59
00:03:28.506 --> 00:03:32.899
isPrinted has a contract: a true answer means this is a Printed.

60
00:03:32.899 --> 00:03:35.370
The lying version just returns true.

61
00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.664
It's a clerk who stamps every envelope unopened.

62
00:03:38.664 --> 00:03:39.693
Very fast work.

63
00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.738
So what happens: a compile error, a warning, or a failure at run time?

64
00:03:44.738 --> 00:03:45.844
Pause and guess.

65
00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:51.657
It compiles without a single warning.

66
00:03:51.657 --> 00:03:54.351
Then, at run time: ClassCastException.

67
00:03:54.351 --> 00:03:56.974
A Jammed cannot be cast to a Printed.

68
00:03:56.974 --> 00:03:57.470
Well...

69
00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.243
the stamp said otherwise.

70
00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:02.234
One level down, it's plain.

71
00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:04.980
javap prints the bytecode the JVM runs.

72
00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:12.301
Right after the call to isPrinted sits a checkcast to Printed that nobody wrote by hand: the smart cast.

73
00:04:12.301 --> 00:04:16.243
The contract is only extra data for the Kotlin compiler.

74
00:04:16.243 --> 00:04:18.777
The running program never checks it.

75
00:04:19.867 --> 00:04:26.239
The design document for contracts says it plainly: the compiler trusts contracts unconditionally.

76
00:04:26.239 --> 00:04:28.998
A contract can't make a wrong value right.

77
00:04:28.998 --> 00:04:33.071
It only moves the cast into a promise inside another function.

78
00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:35.168
So, three rules.

79
00:04:35.168 --> 00:04:41.361
Keep the body as simple as the promise, the way require does, so a reader can check it at a glance.

80
00:04:41.361 --> 00:04:44.801
Put the contract first: anything before it is an error.

81
00:04:44.801 --> 00:04:47.804
And opt in knowing the feature can still change.

82
00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.130
Here the shop picture is kinder than the compiler.

83
00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:56.394
A careful clerk might peek inside a stamped envelope now and then.

84
00:04:56.394 --> 00:04:57.944
The compiler never does.

85
00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:02.361
So, why can't my check function do what require does?

86
00:05:02.361 --> 00:05:04.370
It can, once it carries a stamp.

87
00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:07.698
The compiler smart-casts only from checks it can see.

88
00:05:07.698 --> 00:05:13.097
A contract writes the check on the outside, and the compiler believes it, true or not.

89
00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.270
Quick check, for your own code.

90
00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:21.625
A call to your own check function, and on the very next line, two exclamation marks after the value.

91
00:05:21.625 --> 00:05:22.579
What's missing?

92
00:05:25.767 --> 00:05:26.454
A contract.

93
00:05:26.454 --> 00:05:31.078
Those two marks are you telling the compiler what your check already knew.

94
00:05:31.078 --> 00:05:33.765
Or the check could simply return the value.
